Z||000000||ragilmore||10-19-2001||05:22 PM||||My 88 formula v6 had a broken pin on sending unit causing gage to show max presser changed to after market unit no help same problem yes I did remove the broken pin from plug Any help? Thank's Richard||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||10-19-2001||06:10 PM||||The center pin on the oil pressure sending unit is the (only) one that talks to the gage on the instrument panel.  To check the  wiring + gage, leave the connector unplugged, and turn the ignition switch on.  The O.P. gage should peg "high" (max. pressure).  Next, carefully jumper the center pin on the connector to ground.  The gage should drop to zero pressure.<P>If the above checks out, the gage + wiring are okay.<P>You say your gage pegs "high"; that tends to indicate there's an "open" in the wiring to the center terminal, the body of the sender isn't grounded, or a bad terminal in the connector's center pin (i.e. - it's not making the connection to the sender).<P>It amounts to: If there's 90 ohms or <B>more</B> resistance from the center terminal to ground, the O.P. gage will indicate max. pressure.  If there's <B>zero</B> ohms (shorted to ground), the O.P. gage should show zero pressure.<P>That's all I know.  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   <P>    ||||reg||1
Z||000002||JetroGT||10-20-2001||09:10 AM||||When I changed the sensor it stayed peged for a few miles then went back to normal. I had it unpluged for a few days.. Oops.. I used a GM sensor if that makes any difference.||||reg||1
